<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <!--
      ================================================================================================
      REFERENCE / TEMPLATE POM — Open Processors.

      This is not a buildable module pom; it is the canonical starting point for a NEW gold-standard
      OP and the diff baseline for a consistency review of an EXISTING one. Copy this file to
      <NewOp>/pom.xml and customize the points marked "CUSTOMIZE" below (base name, OP_VERSION,
      description, op-specific runtime deps). Everything else — the release-parameterization
      structure (STRIIM_HOME / STRIIM_VERSION / STRIIM_SERIES / JAVA_RELEASE, the frozen third-party
      lib versions, the striim-home-from-env / release-from-env profiles), the system-scoped Striim
      dependency block, the shade/surefire plugin configuration, and the manifest shape — is the
      uniform gold-standard shape and should NOT vary op to op without updating this reference (and
      docs/TESTING-YOUR-JAVA.md) in the same change.

      TWO KINDS OF "CUSTOMIZE" MARKER, and a mechanical reconcile has to tell them apart:

        CUSTOMIZE            — a point every module MUST fill in with its own value (base name,
                               OP_VERSION, description, op-specific runtime deps).
        CUSTOMIZE (optional) — a dependency a module includes ONLY IF IT USES IT. A module that
                               omits one is CONFORMANT, not divergent. A dependency review compares this file structurally, and a structural diff
                               cannot see the prose explaining a block, so an unmarked optional
                               dependency reads as a gap in every module that correctly omits it.
                               Today: joda-time and org.json. The condition is USE, not
                               convention.

      The OP version-consistency rules (package suffix == OP_VERSION == @PropertyTemplate name ==
      artifactId leaf) must remain aligned; the pom settings below support that consistency.

      Uses a placeholder base name "MyOp". Replace "MyOp" throughout with the real
      op's base name (matching its package/class name), and "V1" with its initial OP_VERSION.
      ================================================================================================
    -->
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <groupId>com.example</groupId>
    <!-- CUSTOMIZE: <Base><OP_VERSION>, e.g. MyOpV1. == the package leaf (com.example.MyOpV1);
         the version suffix (V1) is the Striim OP-cache key. Keep artifactId, name, the code package, the
         @PropertyTemplate(name=...), and OP_VERSION in agreement whenever the version is bumped. -->
    <artifactId>MyOpV1</artifactId>
    <packaging>jar</packaging>
    <version>1.0.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    <name>MyOpV1</name>
    <!-- CUSTOMIZE: one-line description of what this OP does. -->
    <description>Striim Open Processor for &lt;what this op does&gt;</description>

    <properties>
        <!-- Reproducible build. Without this, maven-jar-plugin and maven-shade-plugin stamp
             every zip entry with the moment of the build, so `mvn clean package` over an
             UNCHANGED tree emits a different sha256 every time. The live framework keys its
             OP registry on that digest, so any rebuild looks like a changed jar and forces
             an UNLOAD + LOAD OPEN PROCESSOR: a cluster-wide reload nobody asked for, and the
             operation that wedges Striim's OP loader. A fixed value makes the artifact a pure
             function of its inputs. Bump it when you want the stamp to move, never per build.
             Keep the timestamp fixed for reproducible output. -->
        <project.build.outputTimestamp>2026-01-01T00:00:00Z</project.build.outputTimestamp>
        <project.build.sourceEncoding>UTF-8</project.build.sourceEncoding>

        <!-- CUSTOMIZE: bump the letter for each shippable iteration (V1, V1A, V2, ...). Must match
             the code package suffix, the @PropertyTemplate(name=...), and the artifactId leaf above. -->
        <OP_VERSION>V1</OP_VERSION>

        <!-- THE INTEGRATION LAYER IS REQUIRED. Every OP MUST add-source OpenProcessorCommon
             (the build-helper block below) and reach the platform through it. A module without
             that wiring has no way to satisfy the rule that follows, so this is the first
             requirement, not a consequence of it.

             PLATFORM COUPLING. These system-scoped jars belong to the platform and are NOT
             shaded into the module: outside a real Striim server they are simply ABSENT, so a
             class loaded from them raises NoClassDefFoundError, an Error that
             `catch (Exception)` does not catch. Reference com.webaction.* from src/main ONLY
             where no shared seam exists.

             Keep platform access behind the shared interfaces listed below so
             the integration harness can substitute them.

             Everything else routes through
             OpenProcessorCommon, add-sourced below:
               MetadataAccess           MDCache, MetadataRepository, SecurityInfo,
                                        runtime.Context, uuid.AuthToken
               TypeResolver             runtime.compiler.*, runtime.TypeGenerator
               BuiltInFuncs             runtime.BuiltInFunc
               PositionPropagation      hand-rolled position attachment
               SecretResolver           decrypting a credential yourself
               AbstractOpenProcessorApp openprocessor.StriimOpenProcessor

             A hand-rolled platform call also cannot be unit tested: a static getInstance()
             cannot be mocked. If no seam exists for what you need, that is a finding. Propose
             one; do not add another hand-roll. A platform lint can check this.
             -->
        <!-- Path to the Striim install whose lib/ supplies the system-scoped jars below. The default
             here must stay the neutral /opt/striim — NEVER a personal path — and is overridden by a
             STRIIM_HOME environment variable (the striim-home-from-env profile picks it up) or a CLI
             -DSTRIIM_HOME=... (which beats both). -->
        <STRIIM_HOME>/opt/striim</STRIIM_HOME>

        <!-- Release triad the module builds against by default. These three, plus the frozen
             third-party lib versions below, are driven from your STRIIM_HOME install via the
             release-from-env profile: exporting STRIIM_RELEASE (+ the
             triad) derived from the install, and building with it set overrides all three together so they
             always resolve in lockstep. The defaults here (the
             5.4.x baseline) keep the module standalone-buildable with no env set at all. -->
        <STRIIM_VERSION>5.4.0.6</STRIIM_VERSION>
        <STRIIM_SERIES>5.4</STRIIM_SERIES>
        <JAVA_RELEASE>17</JAVA_RELEASE>

        <!-- Transitive Striim runtime libs: frozen across releases (same jar in every Striim
             install's lib/), so these have NO release-from-env override
             — the property default always applies. Only add the ones this op actually needs on its
             classpath (WAEvent-touching code typically needs all of these; a pure-compute op may need
             fewer). If a NEW release ever moves one of these, export it alongside the triad AND
             give it its own from-env override profile here (see the commented mssqljdbc-from-env
             example below) rather than folding it into release-from-env. -->
        <HAZELCAST_VERSION>5.3.5</HAZELCAST_VERSION>
        <KRYO_VERSION>2.20</KRYO_VERSION>
        <JACKSON_VERSION>2.14.0</JACKSON_VERSION>
        <JETTISON_VERSION>1.5.4</JETTISON_VERSION>
        <JODA_TIME_VERSION>2.10.13</JODA_TIME_VERSION>
        <JSON_VERSION>20220320</JSON_VERSION>
        <LOG4J_VERSION>2.17.2</LOG4J_VERSION>
        <!-- Other frozen libs some gold ops need on the system-scoped classpath (add only if used):
             JEROMQ_VERSION (0.5.1 — SourceProcess base hierarchy touches zmq.* at class-link time). -->

        <maven.compiler.source>${JAVA_RELEASE}</maven.compiler.source>
        <maven.compiler.target>${JAVA_RELEASE}</maven.compiler.target>
    </properties>

    <dependencies>

        <!-- ================================================================ -->
        <!-- Striim runtime (system scope — provided by the Striim server).   -->
        <!-- Declared explicitly from the Striim lib/ rather than pulled from -->
        <!-- the OpenProcessorSDK fat jar, which bundles a STALE WAEvent that -->
        <!-- shadows Common's on the test classpath.                          -->
        <!-- ================================================================ -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.webaction</groupId>
            <artifactId>Platform</artifactId>
            <version>${STRIIM_VERSION}</version>
            <scope>system</scope>
            <systemPath>${STRIIM_HOME}/lib/Platform-${STRIIM_VERSION}.jar</systemPath>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.webaction</groupId>
            <artifactId>Common</artifactId>
            <version>${STRIIM_VERSION}</version>
            <scope>system</scope>
            <systemPath>${STRIIM_HOME}/lib/Common-${STRIIM_VERSION}.jar</systemPath>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.webaction</groupId>
            <artifactId>SourceCommons</artifactId>
            <version>${STRIIM_VERSION}</version>
            <scope>system</scope>
            <systemPath>${STRIIM_HOME}/lib/SourceCommons-${STRIIM_VERSION}.jar</systemPath>
        </dependency>

        <!-- Hazelcast — required explicitly in Striim 5.4.x. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.hazelcast</groupId>
            <artifactId>hazelcast</artifactId>
            <version>${HAZELCAST_VERSION}</version>
            <scope>system</scope>
            <systemPath>${STRIIM_HOME}/lib/hazelcast-${HAZELCAST_VERSION}.jar</systemPath>
        </dependency>

        <!-- Jackson — already on the Striim server classpath; used for ORM config parsing. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.fasterxml.jackson.core</groupId>
            <artifactId>jackson-databind</artifactId>
            <version>${JACKSON_VERSION}</version>
            <scope>system</scope>
            <systemPath>${STRIIM_HOME}/lib/jackson-databind-${JACKSON_VERSION}.jar</systemPath>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.fasterxml.jackson.core</groupId>
            <artifactId>jackson-core</artifactId>
            <version>${JACKSON_VERSION}</version>
            <scope>system</scope>
            <systemPath>${STRIIM_HOME}/lib/jackson-core-${JACKSON_VERSION}.jar</systemPath>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.fasterxml.jackson.core</groupId>
            <artifactId>jackson-annotations</artifactId>
            <version>${JACKSON_VERSION}</version>
            <scope>system</scope>
            <systemPath>${STRIIM_HOME}/lib/jackson-annotations-${JACKSON_VERSION}.jar</systemPath>
        </dependency>

        <!-- Kryo — WAEvent implements KryoSerializable; required by Common.jar. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.esotericsoftware</groupId>
            <artifactId>kryo</artifactId>
            <version>${KRYO_VERSION}</version>
            <scope>system</scope>
            <systemPath>${STRIIM_HOME}/lib/kryo-${KRYO_VERSION}.jar</systemPath>
        </dependency>

        <!-- jettison — WAEvent's JSON path references it at construction/runtime; needed on the test
             classpath so WAEvent can be built in tests. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.codehaus.jettison</groupId>
            <artifactId>jettison</artifactId>
            <version>${JETTISON_VERSION}</version>
            <scope>system</scope>
            <systemPath>${STRIIM_HOME}/lib/jettison-${JETTISON_VERSION}.jar</systemPath>
        </dependency>

        <!-- CUSTOMIZE (optional): joda-time — DateTime-typed WAEvent columns/metadata use
             org.joda.time.DateTime. Omit if this op never touches a DateTime-typed column.
             A module that omits it is CONFORMANT, not divergent — see the header. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>joda-time</groupId>
            <artifactId>joda-time</artifactId>
            <version>${JODA_TIME_VERSION}</version>
            <scope>system</scope>
            <systemPath>${STRIIM_HOME}/lib/joda-time-${JODA_TIME_VERSION}.jar</systemPath>
        </dependency>

        <!-- CUSTOMIZE (optional): org.json — only needed if the op navigates JSONObject/JSONArray
             (e.g. a REST API response). Omit if unused. A module that omits it is CONFORMANT,
             not divergent — see the header. -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.json</groupId>
            <artifactId>json</artifactId>
            <version>${JSON_VERSION}</version>
            <scope>system</scope>
            <systemPath>${STRIIM_HOME}/lib/json-${JSON_VERSION}.jar</systemPath>
        </dependency>

        <!-- ================================================================ -->
        <!-- CUSTOMIZE: op-specific runtime deps bundled into the shaded jar  -->
        <!-- (normal compile-scope deps — NOT system-scoped, NOT on the       -->
        <!-- Striim server classpath). Example shown; delete/replace.        -->
        <!-- ================================================================ -->
        <!--
        <dependency>
            <groupId>com.example</groupId>
            <artifactId>example-client</artifactId>
            <version>1.2.3</version>
        </dependency>
        -->

        <!-- ================================================================ -->
        <!-- Test scope                                                       -->
        <!-- ================================================================ -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
            <artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId>
            <version>5.10.0</version>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.mockito</groupId>
            <artifactId>mockito-core</artifactId>
            <version>5.18.0</version>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>net.bytebuddy</groupId>
            <artifactId>byte-buddy-agent</artifactId>
            <version>1.17.6</version>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.apache.logging.log4j</groupId>
            <artifactId>log4j-api</artifactId>
            <version>${LOG4J_VERSION}</version>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.apache.logging.log4j</groupId>
            <artifactId>log4j-core</artifactId>
            <version>${LOG4J_VERSION}</version>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.apache.logging.log4j</groupId>
            <artifactId>log4j-slf4j-impl</artifactId>
            <version>${LOG4J_VERSION}</version>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>

    </dependencies>

    <profiles>
        <!-- Override STRIIM_HOME from a STRIIM_HOME environment variable when set; otherwise the
             default in <properties> is used. A CLI -DSTRIIM_HOME=... overrides both. -->
        <profile>
            <id>striim-home-from-env</id>
            <activation>
                <property>
                    <name>env.STRIIM_HOME</name>
                </property>
            </activation>
            <properties>
                <STRIIM_HOME>${env.STRIIM_HOME}</STRIIM_HOME>
            </properties>
        </profile>

        <!-- Override the release triad from env vars when STRIIM_RELEASE is set — the marker
             exported alongside STRIIM_VERSION/STRIIM_SERIES/JAVA_RELEASE,
             so all three resolve together (a dedicated marker so a stray STRIIM_VERSION in the shell
             can't misfire the build). Frozen transitive libs (hazelcast, kryo, jackson, jettison,
             joda-time, json, log4j) are NOT overridden here — same jar across releases, so the
             property default always applies. A lib that DOES move per release (e.g. mssql-jdbc) gets
             its own independent from-env profile instead — see the commented example below. -->
        <profile>
            <id>release-from-env</id>
            <activation>
                <property>
                    <name>env.STRIIM_RELEASE</name>
                </property>
            </activation>
            <properties>
                <STRIIM_VERSION>${env.STRIIM_VERSION}</STRIIM_VERSION>
                <STRIIM_SERIES>${env.STRIIM_SERIES}</STRIIM_SERIES>
                <JAVA_RELEASE>${env.JAVA_RELEASE}</JAVA_RELEASE>
            </properties>
        </profile>

        <!-- EXAMPLE — per-lib override profile for a lib whose version moves independently of the
             release triad (a Striim install's bundled driver version doesn't necessarily move in
             lockstep with the Striim release). Uncomment and adapt when this op has such a
             dependency; add a matching <MSSQL_JDBC_VERSION> property + system-scoped dependency
             above, and export MSSQL_JDBC_VERSION where it differs from the
             pom default.
        <profile>
            <id>mssqljdbc-from-env</id>
            <activation>
                <property>
                    <name>env.MSSQL_JDBC_VERSION</name>
                </property>
            </activation>
            <properties>
                <MSSQL_JDBC_VERSION>${env.MSSQL_JDBC_VERSION}</MSSQL_JDBC_VERSION>
            </properties>
        </profile>
        -->
    </profiles>

    <build>
        <!-- Set at the <build> level (not only in the shade plugin) so the implicit
             default-jar and the shade goal target the SAME filename — otherwise default-jar
             emits a stray ${artifactId}-1.0.0-SNAPSHOT.jar beside the shaded jar. -->
        <finalName>${project.artifactId}-${STRIIM_SERIES}</finalName>
        <plugins>

            <!-- ================================================================================
                 OpenProcessorCommon add-source. Adds the shared gold module's sources
                 (java/OpenProcessors/OpenProcessorCommon/src/main/java, package
                 com.example.common — the ten shared types: EventProcessor, Emitter,
                 BuiltInFuncs, BuiltInFuncResolver, TypeResolver, MdrTypeResolver,
                 BuiltInFuncTypeCompileCallback, Logger, PositionPropagation,
                 AbstractOpenProcessorApp) to THIS
                 op's compile sourceRoot, so they compile and shade into the single OP jar
                 alongside this op's own classes — no separate install step, no reactor ordering;
                 OpenProcessorCommon sources compile with the consuming module.

                 Consequence: this op does NOT carry its own Logger.java, platform-seam
                 (TypeResolver/PlatformContext/Mdr*TypeResolver) class, Emitter.java, or App
                 start/run/close boilerplate — those are REMOVED from the per-op source tree and
                 live ONLY in OpenProcessorCommon. An in-stream op's App extends
                 OpenProcessorCommon's AbstractOpenProcessorApp (a pull-style reader/source instead
                 extends com.webaction.proc.SourceProcess and uses the shared Emitter seam — see
                 docs/TESTING-YOUR-JAVA.md "pick the right base"); its Processor implements
                 EventProcessor<WAEvent> and takes the shared Logger (+ optionally the shared
                 BuiltInFuncs and/or TypeResolver) by ONE constructor — exactly one declared
                 constructor, which the scripts/integration harness enforces reflectively.
                 ================================================================================ -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.codehaus.mojo</groupId>
                <artifactId>build-helper-maven-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>3.6.0</version>
                <executions>
                    <execution>
                        <id>add-openprocessorcommon-source</id>
                        <phase>generate-sources</phase>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>add-source</goal>
                        </goals>
                        <configuration>
                            <sources>
                                <source>${project.basedir}/../OpenProcessorCommon/src/main/java</source>
                                <!-- Cross-family shared sources (WAEvents).
                                     CUSTOMIZE: keep this root ONLY if the module actually
                                     references a SampleCommon type. Add-sourcing it unused
                                     compiles and shades source the module never calls.
                                     Four of the fleet's OPs keep it; the rest delete it. -->
                                <source>${project.basedir}/../../SampleCommon/src/main/java</source>
                            </sources>
                        </configuration>
                    </execution>
                </executions>
            </plugin>

            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-compiler-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>3.13.0</version>
                <configuration>
                    <source>${maven.compiler.source}</source>
                    <target>${maven.compiler.target}</target>
                </configuration>
            </plugin>

            <!-- PINNED to 3.2.5: an unpinned surefire silently falls back to 2.12.4, which predates
                 JUnit 5 and reports a vacuous "Tests run: 0" green. Do not remove the pin. -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>3.2.5</version>
            </plugin>

            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-shade-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>3.5.1</version>
                <configuration>
                    <createDependencyReducedPom>false</createDependencyReducedPom>
                    <!-- Relocate the shared packages to a module-unique name: Striim's global
                         LOAD rejects two jars declaring the same class. -->
                    <relocations>
                        <relocation>
                            <pattern>com.example.common</pattern>
                            <shadedPattern>com.example.shaded.${project.artifactId}.common</shadedPattern>
                        </relocation>
                        <!-- IF THIS OP BUNDLES ANY THIRD-PARTY LIBRARY, RELOCATE IT TOO.
                             Striim's class loading is PARENT-FIRST: ModuleClassLoader.loadClass
                             falls through to URLClassLoader.loadClass(name, false), and
                             URLClassLoader does not override loadClass, so it lands on
                             ClassLoader.loadClass. Only "org.slf4j." is special-cased. The
                             server's classpath is conf:lib/*, so THE SERVER WINS every package
                             you share with it — your bundled copy is shadowed, whatever version
                             you pinned.

                             MEASURED (2026-08-28, JdbcSink): Striim ships
                             protobuf-java 2.5.0 and guava 31.1-jre, and snowflake-jdbc bundles
                             its own com.google.api and io.grpc. A Spanner client compiled against
                             protobuf 3.x linked its generated <clinit> against 2.5.0's Descriptors,
                             which lacks the 2-arg internalBuildGeneratedFileFrom — an
                             ExceptionInInitializerError that poisoned Striim's OP class loader.
                             org.apache.commons overlaps 17 of the server's jars.

                             Matching the server's versions is usually impossible (2.5.0 cannot run
                             a modern client) and needs no version property. Rename yours instead:

                        <relocation>
                            <pattern>com.google</pattern>
                            <shadedPattern>com.example.shaded.${project.artifactId}.com.google</shadedPattern>
                            <excludes>
                                <exclude>com.google.cloud.**</exclude>
                            </excludes>
                        </relocation>

                             EXCLUDE any package whose classes load their own resources with
                             Class.getResourceAsStream — the Google Cloud clients do.
                             WALoader.getResourceAsStream serves ONLY ".class" entries out of an OP
                             jar and delegates everything else to a parent whose classpath does not
                             include UploadedFiles, so a relocated resource becomes unreachable and
                             the owning class dies in its static initializer. Excluding is safe when
                             the package does not actually collide with the server's.

                             See JdbcSink/pom.xml for the full worked set. Omit all of
                             this if the op bundles nothing. -->
                    </relocations>
                    <!-- Write the shaded jar directly OVER the default-jar output (same path via
                         the build finalName). outputFile mode does not leave an `original-` backup
                         or a version-named stray, so this is the only unclassified jar in target/
                         (the -sources/-javadoc pair is added below). This is the
                         ${OP_JAR} an example app.tql's LOAD OPEN PROCESSOR "UploadedFiles/${OP_JAR}"
                         references. -->
                    <outputFile>${project.build.directory}/${project.artifactId}-${STRIIM_SERIES}.jar</outputFile>
                    <transformers>
                        <transformer implementation="org.apache.maven.plugins.shade.resource.ManifestResourceTransformer">
                            <manifestEntries>
                                <!-- CUSTOMIZE: "MyOp" below to the real base name. Both entries carry
                                     ${OP_VERSION} so a version bump only requires bumping the property. -->
                                <Striim-Module-Name>MyOp${OP_VERSION}</Striim-Module-Name>
                                <Striim-Service-Interface>com.webaction.runtime.components.openprocessor.StriimOpenProcessor</Striim-Service-Interface>
                                <Striim-Service-Implementation>com.example.MyOp${OP_VERSION}.App</Striim-Service-Implementation>
                                <!-- Release the jar was built against; the live-test harness reads
                                     these to refuse reusing a jar from a different release. -->
                                <Striim-Build-Version>${STRIIM_VERSION}</Striim-Build-Version>
                                <Striim-Build-Series>${STRIIM_SERIES}</Striim-Build-Series>
                                <Striim-Build-Java>${JAVA_RELEASE}</Striim-Build-Java>
                            </manifestEntries>
                        </transformer>
                        <!-- Merge META-INF/services across any bundled dep stack that resolves
                             implementations via ServiceLoader (e.g. google-cloud / gRPC / gax).
                             Omit this transformer if the op bundles no such dep. -->
                        <transformer implementation="org.apache.maven.plugins.shade.resource.ServicesResourceTransformer"/>
                    </transformers>
                    <artifactSet>
                        <excludes>
                            <exclude>org.slf4j:*</exclude>
                            <exclude>log4j:*</exclude>
                        </excludes>
                    </artifactSet>
                    <!-- Strip signature files inherited from any signed bundled dependency (once the
                         uber-jar is repackaged their digests no longer match, which makes the JVM
                         reject the jar with "Invalid signature file digest for Manifest main
                         attributes"). Also drop the versioned module-info.class, meaningless in an
                         uber-jar. Omit this filter if the op bundles no signed dependency. -->
                    <filters>
                        <filter>
                            <artifact>*:*</artifact>
                            <excludes>
                                <!-- Both forms, per the shade filters below: module-info.class causes
                                     classloader conflicts on Java 17, and the versions/9-only pattern
                                     misses versions/11 and versions/17 in a multi-release jar. -->
                                <exclude>module-info.class</exclude>
                                <exclude>META-INF/versions/*/module-info.class</exclude>
                                <exclude>META-INF/*.SF</exclude>
                                <exclude>META-INF/*.DSA</exclude>
                                <exclude>META-INF/*.RSA</exclude>
                                <exclude>META-INF/*.EC</exclude>
                            </excludes>
                        </filter>
                    </filters>
                </configuration>
                <executions>
                    <execution>
                        <phase>package</phase>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>shade</goal>
                        </goals>
                    </execution>
                </executions>
            </plugin>

            <!-- ================================================================================
                 API docs and sources, bound to `package`. Output is named from this module's jar
                 base name, landing beside it as <jar>-sources.jar and <jar>-javadoc.jar.

                 Why bind rather than pass the goals on the command line: javadoc is only checked
                 when it is generated, and `mvn package` is the command a developer runs. A broken
                 reference fails that build; so does a @param naming an argument the method does
                 not have. A MISSING @param or @return is only a warning, which is deliberate,
                 because the templates require neither. The check is of form, never of truth: a
                 green build proves the tags RESOLVE, not that they are correct.

                 Do NOT also pass these goals on the command line where this pom is built. The
                 lifecycle already runs them, and a second `default-cli` execution regenerates and
                 re-jars the javadoc for nothing.

                 Both versions are PINNED for determinism, and as a floor against a future Maven 4
                 runner. The pin does not change which version resolves today: 4.0.0-beta-1 declares
                 a Maven 4 prerequisite, so Maven 3.9 rejects it and selects 3.4.0 unaided. Unlike
                 the surefire pin, which prevents a real and current silent-zero-tests green, this
                 one is insurance.

                 These two jars are DEVELOPER artifacts and are never shipped:
                 a packaging step should drop any -sources/-javadoc jar from a customer package.
                 target/ therefore holds more than one jar,
                 so anything counting jars there must exclude them.
                 The live harness is unaffected: it globs target/*-<series>.jar, which neither suffix
                 can match.
                 ================================================================================ -->
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-source-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>3.4.0</version>
                <executions>
                    <execution>
                        <id>attach-sources</id>
                        <phase>package</phase>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>jar-no-fork</goal>
                        </goals>
                    </execution>
                </executions>
            </plugin>

            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-javadoc-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>3.12.0</version>
                <executions>
                    <execution>
                        <id>attach-javadocs</id>
                        <phase>package</phase>
                        <goals>
                            <goal>jar</goal>
                        </goals>
                    </execution>
                </executions>
            </plugin>

        </plugins>
    </build>
</project>
